1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,120
We've been discussing the factors the architect has to consider when designing domestic buildings.

2
00:00:08,120 --> 00:00:13,240
I'm going to move on now to consider the design of public buildings,

3
00:00:13,240 --> 00:00:19,523
and I'll illustrate this by referring to the new Taylor Concert Hall that's recently

4
00:00:19,523 --> 00:00:21,410
been completed here in the city.

5
00:00:21,410 --> 00:00:25,061
So, as with a domestic building,

6
00:00:25,061 --> 00:00:30,999
when designing a public building, an architect needs to consider the function of the

7
00:00:30,999 --> 00:00:31,420
building

8
00:00:31,420 --> 00:00:36,177
for example, is it to be used primarily for entertainment,

9
00:00:36,177 --> 00:00:38,064
or for education,

10
00:00:38,064 --> 00:00:39,850
or for administration?

11
00:00:39,850 --> 00:00:45,658
The second thing the architect needs to think about is the context of the

12
00:00:45,658 --> 00:00:46,160
building,

13
00:00:46,160 --> 00:00:49,799
this includes its physical location, obviously,

14
00:00:49,799 --> 00:00:53,800
but it also includes the social meaning of the building,

15
00:00:53,800 --> 00:00:56,690
how it relates to the people it's built for.

16
00:00:56,690 --> 00:01:03,255
And finally, for an important public buildings, the architect may also be looking for

17
00:01:03,255 --> 00:01:07,370
a centralsymbolic idea on which to base the design,

18
00:01:07,370 --> 00:01:11,445
a sort of metaphor for the building and the way in which it is

19
00:01:11,445 --> 00:01:12,090
used.

20
00:01:12,090 --> 00:01:17,660
Let's look at the new Taylor Concert Hall in relation to these ideas.

21
00:01:17,660 --> 00:01:23,422
The location chosen was a site in a run-down district that has been ignored

22
00:01:23,422 --> 00:01:25,370
in previous redevelopment plans.

23
00:01:25,370 --> 00:01:29,760
It was occupied by a factory that had been empty for some years.

24
00:01:29,760 --> 00:01:36,506
The whole area was some distance from the high-rise office blocks of the central business district

25
00:01:36,506 --> 00:01:37,650
and shopping centre,

26
00:01:37,650 --> 00:01:40,950
but it was only one kilometre from the ring road.

27
00:01:40,950 --> 00:01:45,930
The site itself was bordered to the north by a canal which had once

28
00:01:45,930 --> 00:01:50,227
been used by boats bringing in raw materials when the area was used for

29
00:01:50,227 --> 00:01:51,070
manufacturing.

30
00:01:51,070 --> 00:01:56,220
The architect chosen for the project was Tom Harrison.

31
00:01:56,220 --> 00:02:01,257
He found the main design challenge was the location of the site in an

32
00:02:01,257 --> 00:02:04,950
area that had no neighbouring buildings of any importance.

33
00:02:04,950 --> 00:02:10,230
To reflect the fact that the significance of the building in this quite run-down

34
00:02:10,230 --> 00:02:12,820
location was as yet unknown,

35
00:02:12,820 --> 00:02:18,518
he decided to create a building centred around the idea of a mystery -

36
00:02:18,518 --> 00:02:22,190
something whose meaning still has to be discovered.

37
00:02:22,190 --> 00:02:27,020
So how was this reflected in the design of the building?

38
00:02:27,020 --> 00:02:32,384
Well, Harrison decided to create pedestrian access to the building

39
00:02:32,384 --> 00:02:36,060
and to make use of the presence of water on the site.

40
00:02:36,060 --> 00:02:41,520
As people approach the entrance, they therefore have to cross over a bridge.

41
00:02:41,520 --> 00:02:46,500
He wanted to give people a feeling of suspense as they see the building

42
00:02:46,500 --> 00:02:47,825
first from a distance,

43
00:02:47,825 --> 00:02:49,010
and then close-up,

44
00:02:49,010 --> 00:02:53,570
and the initial impression he wanted to create from the shape of the building

45
00:02:53,570 --> 00:02:55,840
as a whole was that of a box.

46
00:02:55,840 --> 00:03:01,747
The first side that people see, the southern wall, is just a high, flat

47
00:03:01,747 --> 00:03:04,440
wall uninterrupted by any windows.

48
00:03:04,440 --> 00:03:06,752
This might sound off-putting,

49
00:03:06,752 --> 00:03:10,050
but it supports Harrison's concept of the building

50
00:03:10,050 --> 00:03:15,040
that the person approaching is intrigued and wonders what will be inside.

51
00:03:15,040 --> 00:03:18,970
And this flat wall also has another purpose.

52
00:03:18,970 --> 00:03:25,456
At night-time, projectors are switched on and it functions as a huge screen, onto

53
00:03:25,456 --> 00:03:27,350
which images are projected.

54
00:03:27,350 --> 00:03:35,510
The auditorium itself seats 1500 people.

55
00:03:35,510 --> 00:03:39,340
The floor's supported by ten massive pads.

56
00:03:39,340 --> 00:03:41,367
These are constructed from rubber,

57
00:03:41,367 --> 00:03:45,422
and so are able to absorb any vibrations from outside

58
00:03:45,422 --> 00:03:48,260
and prevent them from affecting the auditorium.

59
00:03:48,260 --> 00:03:54,359
The walls are made of several layers of honey-coloured wood, all sourced from local

60
00:03:54,359 --> 00:03:55,230
beech trees.

61
00:03:55,230 --> 00:03:59,339
In order to improve the acoustic properties of the auditorium

62
00:03:59,339 --> 00:04:04,270
and to amplify the sound, they are not straight, they are curved.

63
00:04:04,270 --> 00:04:08,578
The acoustics are also adjustable according to the size of orchestra

64
00:04:08,578 --> 00:04:11,320
and the type of music being played.

65
00:04:11,320 --> 00:04:17,200
In order to achieve this, there are nine movable panels in the ceiling above

66
00:04:17,200 --> 00:04:20,140
the orchestra which are all individually motorized,

67
00:04:20,140 --> 00:04:24,184
and the walls also have curtains which can be opened

68
00:04:24,184 --> 00:04:26,610
or closed to change the acoustics.

69
00:04:26,610 --> 00:04:32,270
The reaction of the public to the new building has generally been positive.

70
00:04:32,270 --> 00:04:37,800
However, the evaluation of some critics has been less enthusiastic.

71
00:04:37,800 --> 00:04:43,917
In spite of Harrison s efforts to use local materials, they criticise the style

72
00:04:43,917 --> 00:04:47,850
of the design as being international rather than local,

73
00:04:47,850 --> 00:04:54,106
and say it doesn't reflect features of the landscape or society for which it

74
00:04:54,106 --> 00:04:55,000
is built.
